WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.349 --> 00:00:11.610
PAUL JAY: Welcome to The Real News Network.
I'm Paul Jay. And welcome to Reality Asserts

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:12.740
Itself.

00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:18.640
John Kiriakou is in his house waiting for
another 40 days before he's free to walk out

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:24.180
the front door. He's under house arrest after
spending 23 months in jail.

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:29.800
John Kiriakou was born on August 9, 1964.
He's a former CIA analyst and case officer,

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:33.649
a former senior investigator for the Senate
Foreign Relations Committee, and a former

00:00:33.649 --> 00:00:38.230
counterterrorism consultant for ABC News.
He was the first U.S. government official

00:00:38.230 --> 00:00:43.940
to confirm in December 2007 that waterboarding
was used to interrogate al-Qaeda prisoners,

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:50.219
which he described as torture. On October
22, 2012, Kiriakou pleaded guilty to disclosing

00:00:50.219 --> 00:00:55.199
classified information about a fellow CIA
officer that connected the covert operative

00:00:55.199 --> 00:00:59.969
to a specific operation. He was the first
person to pass classified information to a

00:00:59.969 --> 00:01:03.989
reporter, although the reporter did not publish
the name of the operative. He was sentenced

00:01:03.989 --> 00:01:10.220
to 30 months in prison on January 25, 2013,
and he served his term from February 28, 2013,

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:17.220
until 3 February 2015 at a low-security federal
correctional facility in Loretto, Pennsylvania.

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:24.259
When John joined the CIA in 1990, he was 25
years old. He was a true believer in Americanism--American

00:01:24.259 --> 00:01:29.539
exceptionalism, as some people call it. And
now he's not. And that's what this interview's

00:01:29.539 --> 00:01:30.310
about.

00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:31.680
And now John joins us.

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:32.039
Thank you very much.

00:01:32.039 --> 00:01:33.609
JOHN C. KIRIAKOU: Thanks for having me.

00:01:33.609 --> 00:01:38.939
JAY: So we're going to do many segments of
this interview, because you've lived a lot

00:01:38.939 --> 00:01:45.520
of different lives in the CIA. And, I think,
just to establish the ground rules, some of

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:50.850
what we're going to ask John is classified.
And I'm going to not--not not ask him: I'm

00:01:50.850 --> 00:01:54.909
going to ask him whatever we feel like asking
him, and then he's going to figure out if

00:01:54.909 --> 00:01:59.090
there's times he has to say, well, I can't
say that, because it's classified, and then

00:01:59.090 --> 00:02:04.719
he'll parse what he can say. But just to let
you know, that's going to happen once in a

00:02:04.719 --> 00:02:07.950
while--but probably not at the beginning,
'cause I don't think any of you growing up

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:08.649
is classified.

00:02:08.649 --> 00:02:09.250
KIRIAKOU: No.

00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:15.590
JAY: So, in the beginning we want to talk
about how you become Americanized and how

00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:20.250
much that's part of your personal identity.
So your parents are Greek.

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:20.500
KIRIAKOU: Yes.

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:20.910
JAY: You're of Greek origin.

00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:22.030
KIRIAKOU: Yes.

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:25.270
JAY: And when does your family come to the
United States?

00:02:25.270 --> 00:02:30.300
KIRIAKOU: My mother's parents came--I should
say, my father's parents came in February

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:35.670
1931, and my mother's parents followed three
years later.

00:02:35.670 --> 00:02:40.390
Growing up, to hear my grandfather tell the
story, Franklin Roosevelt personally allowed

00:02:40.390 --> 00:02:46.780
him in the country, got him a job in a steel
mill in Canonsburg, Pennsylvania, and by God

00:02:46.780 --> 00:02:51.480
we were going to be Democrats for the rest
of our lives to repay the kindness that Franklin

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:57.020
Roosevelt showed. In fact, into the 1970s,
until my grandfather's death in 1978, he had

00:02:57.020 --> 00:03:01.270
a framed photo of Franklin Roosevelt sitting
on top of the television set.

00:03:01.270 --> 00:03:08.270
So I always grew up with a feeling that I
had to repay the kindness that the country

00:03:08.570 --> 00:03:14.440
had shown my family, that we had a debt to
the Democratic Party, specifically to Franklin

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:19.920
Roosevelt, and that public service was something
to which I should aspire.

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:22.160
JAY: And what did your father and mother do?

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:26.550
KIRIAKOU: My dad was an elementary school
principal for 42 years, and my mother was

00:03:26.550 --> 00:03:27.590
a third grade teacher.

00:03:27.590 --> 00:03:33.400
JAY: And they sort of internalized all this
FDR and America adoration.

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:40.400
KIRIAKOU: Oh, yes, yeah, very much so. Very
much so. Even as a child, I was active politically

00:03:41.390 --> 00:03:47.310
because my parents were. Even as an eight-year-old,
a nine-year-old, I was a--it was even earlier

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:51.980
than that, an eight-year-old--I was a volunteer
on the McGovern campaign, stuffing envelopes

00:03:51.980 --> 00:03:56.600
and such. And then, even when I went to college,
the first thing I did, literally on my first

00:03:56.600 --> 00:03:59.040
day in college, was to join the College Democrats.

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:05.610
JAY: And after Roosevelt, we have Truman.
Did that loyalty transfer on, president to

00:04:05.610 --> 00:04:08.760
president? In other words, did you grow up
with the narrative that dropping the bomb

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:12.520
on Hiroshima and Nagasaki was somehow for
the greater good?

00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:19.449
KIRIAKOU: I remember a relative of mine saying
Truman taught the Japs a thing or two. And

00:04:19.449 --> 00:04:26.449
I'll add, too, that there was at least as
much love for Harry Truman as there was for

00:04:26.680 --> 00:04:32.350
Franklin Roosevelt in our family because it
was Truman that saved Greece from communism.

00:04:32.350 --> 00:04:35.790
And my grandparents were very grateful for
that.

00:04:35.790 --> 00:04:42.630
JAY: And much of Greece was procommunist.
So where did your father, coming in the '30s,

00:04:42.630 --> 00:04:44.590
where did they get their anticommunism from?

00:04:44.590 --> 00:04:50.180
KIRIAKOU: Well, my grandfather was a voracious
consumer of news, which was hard to do in

00:04:50.180 --> 00:04:57.180
the early 1940s. But still he consumed as
much news about Greece as he could, whether

00:04:58.550 --> 00:05:04.760
it was from Life magazine or Time magazine--copies
of which I still have, as a matter fact, that

00:05:04.760 --> 00:05:10.490
mention Greece, or letters to and from relatives
in Greece. He really stayed up on what was

00:05:10.490 --> 00:05:16.000
going on. He was also a member of two Greek-American
organizations--the American Hellenic Educational

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:23.000
Progressive Association (AHEPA) and GAPA (the
Greek American Progressive Association). Both--well,

00:05:23.630 --> 00:05:30.630
I should say GAPA was really a liberal group,
but it supported using Greek, the Greek language,

00:05:30.870 --> 00:05:37.870
in the home. And AHEPA was more pan-political,
but encouraged newly arrived Greeks to assimilate

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:44.010
and to become Americans and to speak English
in the home. My grandfather was a member of

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:45.210
both organizations.

00:05:45.210 --> 00:05:48.490
JAY: Because Roosevelt worked with the Soviet
Union against Hitler,--

00:05:48.490 --> 00:05:49.330
KIRIAKOU: For many years.

00:05:49.330 --> 00:05:54.480
JAY: --Roosevelt's vice president, Wallace,
if he had been able to stay on as vice president

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:59.580
(he got dumped by the party in favor of Truman),
was very much for working with the Soviet

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:04.680
Union, was against this kind of beginning
of a cold war. So, like, moving from Roosevelt

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:08.750
to Truman is not a natural progression in
terms of--especially when it comes to Greece,

00:06:08.750 --> 00:06:12.960
because when you say save Greece from communism,
it was by installing dictators in Greece;

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:15.010
it was not, like, some democracy [incompr.]

00:06:15.010 --> 00:06:19.610
KIRIAKOU: But there was even some disagreement
in the home. My grandmother was very conservative.

00:06:19.610 --> 00:06:23.870
And although she was a Democrat (she became
a Democrat when she became a citizen), she

00:06:23.870 --> 00:06:27.700
was a lifelong royalist when it came to Greek
politics.

00:06:27.700 --> 00:06:29.990
JAY: But was she from the working class or
not?

00:06:29.990 --> 00:06:34.610
KIRIAKOU: Oh, yes, very much so, very much
so. Her mother died at a young age, and her

00:06:34.610 --> 00:06:40.389
father believed that his three daughters should
have a trade, have an education. My grandmother

00:06:40.389 --> 00:06:44.340
was the only one of her sisters that went
to school and became a teacher and then a

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:50.250
translator for the Italian occupation government.
She taught Italian soldiers to speak Greek.

00:06:50.250 --> 00:06:57.250
Her sisters were a dressmaker and--oh, her
youngest sister ended up owning a gas station

00:06:58.590 --> 00:07:05.590
and a butcher shop. So there was always this
drive to succeed, even though they were women,

00:07:06.310 --> 00:07:07.780
that my great-grandfather had instilled.

00:07:07.780 --> 00:07:13.650
JAY: So, in the narrative of your families,
when it comes to Greece: that the installation

00:07:13.650 --> 00:07:18.510
of a dictatorship somehow was a good thing,
and somehow that's for democracy.

00:07:18.510 --> 00:07:21.910
KIRIAKOU: It depended on the dictatorship.
My grandparents were really worried about

00:07:21.910 --> 00:07:26.190
communism in Greece in the 1940s because the
communists were killing a lot of innocent

00:07:26.190 --> 00:07:31.010
people. They would go into villages to recruit
men, and any man that didn't want to go back

00:07:31.010 --> 00:07:35.919
up into the mountains to fight for the Greeks
was killed. And oftentimes the wives and children

00:07:35.919 --> 00:07:36.980
were killed as well.

00:07:36.980 --> 00:07:41.680
JAY: Well, I'm not going to argue with you
about it, but I've interviewed Greek communists

00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:45.050
who fought the Nazis and then fought afterwards,
and you get quite a different story.

00:07:45.050 --> 00:07:49.190
KIRIAKOU: Sure. Oh, of course you do. And
still, these three generations later, you're

00:07:49.190 --> 00:07:50.900
still going to get arguments on both sides.

00:07:50.900 --> 00:07:54.639
JAY: Yeah. But at any rate, this is part of
your formation.

00:07:54.639 --> 00:07:56.530
KIRIAKOU: Yeah.

00:07:56.530 --> 00:08:02.389
JAY: America fights for the greater good.
And as I've said on The Real News a few times,

00:08:02.389 --> 00:08:05.139
it's sort of like, we do bad things, but for
good reasons.

00:08:05.139 --> 00:08:10.990
KIRIAKOU: Right. I believed that for many
years, decades. And I don't believe that anymore.

00:08:10.990 --> 00:08:17.990
JAY: So when we get to you in the high school--so
you're born in '64. The Vietnam War's ending

00:08:18.389 --> 00:08:21.610
as you kind of start becoming much more conscious
of politics.

00:08:21.610 --> 00:08:23.180
KIRIAKOU: That's right.

00:08:23.180 --> 00:08:28.699
JAY: As you head into college, what's your
vision of the world and your place in it,

00:08:28.699 --> 00:08:29.550
what you might be doing?

00:08:29.550 --> 00:08:36.550
KIRIAKOU: I considered myself at the time
to be what was then called a social democrat--liberal

00:08:36.949 --> 00:08:43.059
on social issues, economic issues, and then
pretty hawkish on foreign policy and defense.

00:08:43.059 --> 00:08:48.649
I remember there being in retrospect a very
funny debate within the College Democrats

00:08:48.649 --> 00:08:55.639
at GW over the invasion of Grenada: should
we issue a statement condemning the invasion

00:08:55.639 --> 00:09:00.819
of Grenada? These are, like, six college freshmen
and sophomores debating whether we should

00:09:00.819 --> 00:09:07.240
issue a statement like anybody cared. And
I remember opposing a statement, because I

00:09:07.240 --> 00:09:13.389
supported the invasion of Grenada. I don't
recall why, but I did. I felt, I guess, that

00:09:13.389 --> 00:09:18.649
it showed a robust foreign policy and it showed
that we were willing to go out and protect

00:09:18.649 --> 00:09:19.389
our citizens.

00:09:19.389 --> 00:09:20.680
JAY: We needed a win.

00:09:20.680 --> 00:09:26.050
KIRIAKOU: Yeah, we needed a win. It had been
a while since we had had a win. But all through

00:09:26.050 --> 00:09:29.199
college I was pretty hawkish on foreign policy
issues.

00:09:29.199 --> 00:09:34.079
JAY: But you were also--excuse me--in a little
bit weird way, very interested in politics

00:09:34.079 --> 00:09:34.550
and leaders.

00:09:34.550 --> 00:09:34.889
KIRIAKOU: Yes.

00:09:34.889 --> 00:09:38.749
JAY: Didn't you at some point write a letter
to the Shah, and you got a signed, autographed

00:09:38.749 --> 00:09:39.319
picture back? What was that all about?

00:09:39.319 --> 00:09:45.329
KIRIAKOU: I think I was 14 or 15. I wrote
a letter to the Shah of Iran, and I wrote

00:09:45.329 --> 00:09:52.329
to tell him that I was disgusted by the way
our government had treated him, that once

00:09:52.889 --> 00:09:56.439
we had made a decision, we should have stuck
with the decision, and the decision had been

00:09:56.439 --> 00:10:01.740
to allow him to come to the United States
for treatment of his cancer. And when the

00:10:01.740 --> 00:10:06.779
heat got a little more than the president
could handle, the Shah was unceremoniously

00:10:06.779 --> 00:10:12.230
thrown out and ended up in Panama, and then,
later, the Bahamas. So I wrote him a letter

00:10:12.230 --> 00:10:16.300
saying, it's terrible the way you were treated.
And he actually wrote me a letter back saying,

00:10:16.300 --> 00:10:20.160
you're right, it's terrible the way I've been
treated; here's an autographed picture.

00:10:20.160 --> 00:10:22.490
JAY: He probably didn't get many letters like
that.

00:10:22.490 --> 00:10:24.509
KIRIAKOU: No, I'm sure he didn't.

00:10:24.509 --> 00:10:26.399
JAY: What motivated you?

00:10:26.399 --> 00:10:33.399
KIRIAKOU: I don't know. I was just so interested
in foreign affairs that it just seemed like

00:10:34.410 --> 00:10:36.220
a natural thing to do.

00:10:36.220 --> 00:10:40.920
I followed the Iran hostage crisis day by
day by day, for example. And even though Nightline

00:10:40.920 --> 00:10:46.389
was on very late at night for a 14-year-old
or 15-year-old, I stayed up every single night

00:10:46.389 --> 00:10:51.119
and watched Ted Koppel on Nightline, because
originally Nightline was to give us a daily

00:10:51.119 --> 00:10:52.639
update of events in Iran.

00:10:52.639 --> 00:10:56.389
JAY: Yeah, they'd have a day count, every
day, with so many days more and the hostages.

00:10:56.389 --> 00:10:56.639
Yeah.

00:10:56.389 --> 00:11:00.629
KIRIAKOU: That's right. And so I was just
fascinated by it.

00:11:00.629 --> 00:11:05.110
And I remember telling my father--we were
in the car together. And my dad, for whatever

00:11:05.110 --> 00:11:10.019
reason, always wanted me to become a dentist.
And I remember telling him, Dad, I really

00:11:10.019 --> 00:11:13.800
don't want to be a dentist. And he said, well,
what do you want to do? And I said, I think

00:11:13.800 --> 00:11:15.369
I want to join the foreign service.

00:11:15.369 --> 00:11:17.420
JAY: This is at 14.

00:11:17.420 --> 00:11:23.199
KIRIAKOU: I was 14, I guess, at the time.
And he said, well, whatever you want to do,

00:11:23.199 --> 00:11:24.019
just do it well.

00:11:24.019 --> 00:11:25.720
JAY: And you wrote Margaret Thatcher.

00:11:25.720 --> 00:11:26.749
KIRIAKOU: I did.

00:11:26.749 --> 00:11:29.300
JAY: But your choice of leaders are all kind
of the--.

00:11:29.300 --> 00:11:30.339
KIRIAKOU: They were very conservative.

00:11:30.339 --> 00:11:30.589
JAY: Very conservative.

00:11:30.540 --> 00:11:34.529
KIRIAKOU: They were very conservative. I got
an autographed picture from Ezer Weizman,

00:11:34.529 --> 00:11:39.610
at the time the defense minister of Israel.
These are people that today I wouldn't dream

00:11:39.610 --> 00:11:43.559
of writing a letter of support to, but back
then I was pretty hawkish.

00:11:43.559 --> 00:11:47.309
JAY: Was there anyone influencing you in this
direction? Or was it kind of just your own

00:11:47.309 --> 00:11:47.619
thing?

00:11:47.619 --> 00:11:51.439
KIRIAKOU: No, no. It was just--it was my own
thing. It was my own interest.

00:11:51.439 --> 00:11:54.149
JAY: So you go to college.

00:11:54.149 --> 00:11:54.689
KIRIAKOU: Uh-huh.

00:11:54.689 --> 00:11:55.869
JAY: And what do you study?

00:11:55.869 --> 00:11:57.509
KIRIAKOU: Middle Eastern studies.

00:11:57.509 --> 00:11:59.470
JAY: Why?

00:11:59.470 --> 00:12:02.899
KIRIAKOU: I don't know. I was fascinated with
the Middle East.

00:12:02.899 --> 00:12:08.559
A couple of people asked me if I was interested
in Hellenic studies. I could have studied

00:12:08.559 --> 00:12:13.550
at Georgetown or--I mean, there are several
different places that you can study Hellenic

00:12:13.550 --> 00:12:16.949
studies, good schools. And I said, no, I was
more interested in the Middle East. And then

00:12:16.949 --> 00:12:22.629
my dad, in an attempt to get me to stay close
to home, said, well, you know, the University

00:12:22.629 --> 00:12:26.329
of Pittsburgh has a great Eastern European
studies program. And I said, I don't care

00:12:26.329 --> 00:12:31.379
about Eastern European studies, I don't care
about the Soviet Union. I truly didn't. I

00:12:31.379 --> 00:12:35.819
only cared about the Middle East. And there
were three schools in the country at the time

00:12:35.819 --> 00:12:41.689
that had Middle Eastern studies programs:
George Washington, Rutgers, and Brigham Young.

00:12:41.689 --> 00:12:43.809
And George Washington was the easy choice.

00:12:43.809 --> 00:12:50.809
JAY: Now, as [you get] into the late '70s,
even the early '80s, you're coming to consciousness,

00:12:50.929 --> 00:12:56.110
you know, political, you're interested in
foreign affairs. How does--is the revelations

00:12:56.110 --> 00:13:02.610
of what really happened in Vietnam and what
a lie--mostly lies that the war was based

00:13:02.610 --> 00:13:09.059
on--yeah, there's quotes from senior Johnson
officials near the end of the war that if

00:13:09.059 --> 00:13:14.259
70 percent of the reason we're in Vietnam
is just to prove how strong we are, it's now

00:13:14.259 --> 00:13:19.309
become 100 percent of the reason for being
in Vietnam. And you hear that under Nixon,

00:13:19.309 --> 00:13:24.279
that it became just about proving to the world
we can't lose a war, meaning tens of thousands

00:13:24.279 --> 00:13:31.279
of lives--I mean, really, millions of lives,
including perhaps at least 3 million Vietnamese,

00:13:31.309 --> 00:13:36.779
Cambodians, and thousands of American soldiers.
Does that not in some way start to shake your

00:13:36.779 --> 00:13:37.660
Americanism?

00:13:37.660 --> 00:13:44.050
KIRIAKOU: Not at first. In fact, I haven't
thought about this in years, but I remember

00:13:44.050 --> 00:13:50.199
an argument that I got into with a friend
of mine in the College Democrats over Vietnam,

00:13:50.199 --> 00:13:56.009
and I said that I had opposed the war--not
had opposed--I opposed the war. I thought

00:13:56.009 --> 00:14:02.139
it was immoral. I thought it was illegal.
But if I had been drafted, I would have gone.

00:14:02.139 --> 00:14:06.670
And he said if he had been drafted, he would
have gone to Canada. And we had a real argument

00:14:06.670 --> 00:14:11.920
about that. And he said to me, how can you
oppose a war and then go fight in it? And

00:14:11.920 --> 00:14:18.209
I said, because my country asked me to. I
wouldn't give that same answer today, but

00:14:18.209 --> 00:14:20.889
as an 18-year-old that's what I believed.

00:14:20.889 --> 00:14:23.410
JAY: But you did think it was immoral and
illegal,--

00:14:23.410 --> 00:14:23.980
KIRIAKOU: I did.

00:14:23.980 --> 00:14:27.329
JAY: --'cause a lot of the leaders you're
writing to and so on, I mean, they would have

00:14:27.329 --> 00:14:27.749
not said so.

00:14:27.749 --> 00:14:31.249
KIRIAKOU: In fact, I introduced George McGovern
when he announced his candidacy for president

00:14:31.249 --> 00:14:33.299
in September 1984.

00:14:33.299 --> 00:14:35.069
JAY: Yeah, I saw that.

00:14:35.069 --> 00:14:39.720
KIRIAKOU: And in my speech of introduction,
I said that he was one of the few senators

00:14:39.720 --> 00:14:42.759
to stand up to the illegal and immoral war
in Vietnam.

00:14:42.759 --> 00:14:47.369
JAY: That seems a little contradictory with
all the other positions you were taking and

00:14:47.369 --> 00:14:47.619
supporting Grenada.

00:14:47.489 --> 00:14:49.699
KIRIAKOU: When I was a kid, 19 at the time.
Yeah.

00:14:49.699 --> 00:14:51.420
JAY: You were still figuring it out.

00:14:51.420 --> 00:14:55.670
KIRIAKOU: Still figuring it out. We used to
go to the White House at night when you could

00:14:55.670 --> 00:14:58.850
walk right up to the gate and shout--can I
swear on this program?

00:14:58.850 --> 00:15:00.179
JAY: Oh, yes, you can.

00:15:00.179 --> 00:15:04.089
KIRIAKOU: And shout, "Fuck you, Reagan!" you
know, based on something we had seen on the

00:15:04.089 --> 00:15:05.319
news that night.

00:15:05.319 --> 00:15:07.899
JAY: But you supported the invasion of Grenada.

00:15:07.899 --> 00:15:08.559
KIRIAKOU: Yeah, I know.

00:15:08.559 --> 00:15:08.869
JAY: So what were you--.

00:15:08.869 --> 00:15:12.399
KIRIAKOU: I yelled "Fuck you, Reagan" at the
fence, and then I'd go back to the College

00:15:12.399 --> 00:15:17.929
Democrats and say, you know, guys, I've got
to go with Reagan on this one.

00:15:17.929 --> 00:15:24.929
JAY: But still the underlying thing is America
can really do no wrong--maybe mistakes, maybe

00:15:28.480 --> 00:15:28.769
errors.

00:15:28.769 --> 00:15:33.879
KIRIAKOU: That's exactly how I felt. Sure,
we made mistakes, certainly, but our hearts

00:15:33.879 --> 00:15:35.319
were in the right place.

00:15:35.319 --> 00:15:42.319
I really believed that we were that shining
city on a hill. Back then and for many years

00:15:43.899 --> 00:15:48.259
later, really until September 11, I believed
that.

00:15:48.259 --> 00:15:51.029
JAY: Well, we're going to get to that.

00:15:51.029 --> 00:15:55.279
In the continuation of our series of interview
with John, we're going to ask him how he got

00:15:55.279 --> 00:15:57.189
recruited into the CIA.

00:15:57.189 --> 00:16:01.299
Please join us for the next in our series
of interviews on Reality Asserts Itself on

00:16:01.299 --> 00:16:08.299
The Real News Network.

